+\subsection{Why degenerate transitions obstruct this recursion}
+\label{sec:fisher-degenerate}
+
+Consider a state equation with fewer independent shocks than state
+coordinates,
+\begin{align}
+ x_t&=A_\theta x_{t-1}+G_\theta\varepsilon_t,
+ \qquad \varepsilon_t\sim N(0,I_r),\\
+ \mathcal S_j&=A_\theta x_{t-1}^j+\operatorname{range}(G_\theta),
+ \qquad \operatorname{rank}(G_\theta)=r<d_x.
+ \label{eq:degenerate-ancestor-supports}
+\end{align}
+Here $x_t\in\R^{d_x}$, and $\mathcal S_j$ is the set of successors allowed
+by ancestor $j$. Each conditional transition is supported on that affine
+subspace and has no full-dimensional Lebesgue density. Thus
+\eqref{eq:backward-particle-probabilities} cannot be evaluated with ordinary
+ambient Gaussian densities. A pseudoinverse and pseudodeterminant alone do
+not repair it: a rank-aware density must also enforce the correct support.
+
+The AR(2) example in \eqref{eq:ar2-support-distinction} makes the failure
+concrete. A child drawn from ancestor $a$ satisfies
+$x_{t,2}=x_{t-1,1}^a$. At a fixed parameter, conditioning the finite mixture
+on this supporting line excludes every ancestor with a different first
+coordinate. If these coordinates are distinct, the compatible ancestor
+probabilities are
+\begin{equation}
+ b_t^{ji}=\mathbf 1\{j=a\}.
+ \label{eq:degenerate-ancestor-collapse}
+\end{equation}
+The proposed history average then collapses to the sampled genealogy. It
+does not recover the alternative histories needed to repair genealogical
+degeneracy. PaRIS changes how backward ancestors are sampled; it cannot
+give positive probability to these incompatible transitions.
+
+This obstruction concerns the conditional transitions between fixed
+particles. The exact predictive marginal can still be full dimensional
+after integrating over earlier continuous state uncertainty, as the same
+AR(2) example shows. That fact does not make the finite mixture of distinct
+conditional supports full dimensional. An ambient Gaussian proposal assigns
+zero probability to their union, so it cannot provide the exact importance
+correction for that finite singular mixture. Adding full-dimensional process
+noise changes the model; smoothing the finite predictive mixture into an
+ambient KDM changes the approximation. Neither operation establishes the
+displayed model-corrected recursion for the original degenerate transition.
+
+Innovation coordinates are a possible starting point for a different
+derivation. They require the joint likelihood to be written in those
+coordinates, including the initial law and the dependence of the state map
+on $\theta$. Substituting an innovation density for
+$f_\theta(x_t\mid x_{t-1})$ in the existing backward weights is not such a
+derivation. In particular, moving support contributes state-map derivatives
+to the observation factors, as \eqref{eq:moving-support-score} demonstrates.
+
+Some degenerate models admit an exact reduction to common stochastic
+coordinates, or another specially derived smoothing method. The obstruction
+therefore does not rule out every form of smoothing. It does rule out the
+ordinary all-ancestor recursion above as a general method for the degenerate
+DSGE target. A successful full-rank Gaussian comparison would establish no
+extension to that target, whose score construction remains unresolved.
 
